+Aligns 3D Gaussians with 2D semantic features. Choose when semantic consistency is required alongside geometric consistency. [Cross-Modal Alignment Optimization Strategy](19214-cross-modal-alignment-optimization-strategy.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of matched pixel pairs $K$ and the cost of bilinear sampling and eigenvalue decomposition. Eigenvalue decomposition of $3 \times 3$ symmetric matrices is constant time.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing dense parameter maps for all views involved in the matching process.
-*   **Strengths:** Robust to outliers due to Huber loss and confidence weighting. Prevents geometric degeneracy. Improves performance under sparse-view conditions and large camera pose gaps [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires reliable 2D correspondences (e.g., from MASt3R/DINOv3). Performance degrades if correspondences are inaccurate or if confidence estimation is poor [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of matched pairs $K$ and the cost of bilinear sampling and eigenvalue decomposition. Eigenvalue decomposition of $3 \times 3$ matrices is constant time.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing dense parameter maps for all views and the set of matched pairs.
+*   **Strengths:** Robust to outliers due to Huber loss and confidence weighting. Prevents geometric degeneracy (collapsed Gaussians) via the log-eigenvalue gradient property. Improves PSNR and LPIPS in sparse-view settings [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of 2D correspondences from the geometric backbone. If correspondences are poor, the loss may not provide useful gradients.
 *   **When to use:** Sparse-view reconstruction where photometric loss alone is ambiguous.
-*   **When not to use:** Dense-view scenarios with abundant, unambiguous correspondences where photometric loss is sufficient.
+*   **When not to use:** Dense-view settings where photometric loss is sufficient, or when computational resources are extremely limited.
 
 ## Background
-The MAC loss was introduced in "MAC-Splat: Multi-Attribute Consistency for High-Fidelity Sparse-View Reconstruction" [S1]. It builds on the MASt3R geometric backbone and a frozen DINOv3 encoder to obtain semantically informed 2D correspondences [S1]. The method is designed to resolve ambiguities in 3D optimization for sparse-view reconstruction by leveraging semantic information [S1].
+The MAC loss was introduced in "MAC-Splat: Multi-Attribute Consistency for High-Fidelity Sparse-View Reconstruction" [S1]. It builds on the MASt3R geometric backbone and a frozen DINOv3 encoder to obtain semantically informed 2D correspondences [S1]. The method addresses the limitations of conventional losses in sparse-view reconstruction by enforcing direct consistency on 3D Gaussian attributes [S1].
 
